<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chechens</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chechens are a North Caucasian people of the Nakh group, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechnya">Chechnya</a> in the Russian Federation. Largely Muslim since the 16th to 18th centuries, they resisted Russian conquest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasian-war">Caucasian War</a>, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Imam Shamil</a>. In 1944 Stalin deported the whole nation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and in the 1990s and 2000s Chechnya fought two wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chechens.jpg" alt="Chechens" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolai Karlovich Seidlitz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chechens._XIX_century_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chechens are a Northeast Caucasian people of the Nakh group, native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>, who call themselves Nokhchiy. The Nakh peoples have lived in the highlands of the North Caucasus since prehistory, and there is archaeological evidence of continuity from 3000 BCE. The only three surviving Nakh peoples are the Chechens, the Ingush and the Bats. The ancestors of the Chechens and Ingush were known as Durdzuks, and the state of Durdzuketi has been recorded since the 4th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the lowlands were dominated first by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and then by the Alans, and some Chechens adopted Eastern Orthodox Christianity. Islam spread gradually, while the Chechens' pagan religion stayed strong until the 19th century. The Mongol invasions of the 13th century and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a> in the 14th devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechnya">Chechnya</a>. The Chechens never really fell under the rule of the Ottoman or Persian empires.</p><p>In the Caucasian War Russia began a full-scale conquest. General Yermolov used scorched earth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> and founded the fort of Grozny in 1818. Resistance peaked under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Imam Shamil</a> and ended in defeat after decades of war, in which the Chechens lost most of their population.</p><p>In 1944 Stalin ordered all Chechens deported to the Kazakh and Kirghiz republics, and their republic was abolished. At least a quarter, and perhaps half, of the population died. They were rehabilitated in 1956 and allowed to return the next year. Attempts to regain independence after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> led to two wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, beginning in 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Chechen society is largely egalitarian. It is organised around about 130 teips, local clans, grouped in loose confederations called tukkhums. The social code is nokhchallah, loosely Chechen character. Chechens remember their history through the illesh, a collection of epic poems and stories. Their language belongs to the Nakh branch of the Northeast Caucasian family. Most Chechens are Sunni Muslims, and about half belong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> brotherhoods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chechens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The darker legacy includes the Red Terror, collectivisation, the Gulag, the Great Terror of 1937–1938 and the deportation of whole peoples such as the <b>Chechens</b>, Kalmyks, Crimean Tatars and Volga Germans during the Second World War, whose consequences still shape relations in the North Caucasus and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Nationality policy fluctuated between the promotion of local languages and elites in the 1920s, deportations of whole peoples such as the <b>Chechens</b> and Crimean Tatars in the 1940s, and Russification later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1941 to 1949 nearly 3.3 million people were deported to Siberia and Central Asia, among them Volga Germans and <b>Chechens</b>; by some estimates up to 43% of the resettled died of disease and malnutrition.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/stalinism">Stalinism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic received deported peoples, including Koreans, Volga Germans and <b>Chechens</b>, and many Gulag camps such as Karlag.</blockquote><a href="/state/kazakh-ssr">Kazakh SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>After the February Revolution of 1917, a congress at Vladikavkaz founded the Union of United Mountaineers of the North Caucasus and Dagestan, bringing together <b>Chechens</b>, Ingush, Ossetians, Kabardians, Dagestanis and others.</blockquote><a href="/state/mountainous-republic-of-the-northern-caucasus">Mountainous Republic of the Northern Caucasus</a></li><li><blockquote>Persian chroniclers of Timur, notably Sharaf al-Din Yazdi, mention its ruler Ghayur Khan and describe Simsir as a Muslim land, one of the first among the <b>Chechens</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/simsir">Simsir</a></li></ul></section>
